From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: mdio: ipq4019: spin briefly before sleeping on a busy
 bus

ipq4019_mdio_wait_busy() polls the busy bit with readl_poll_timeout()
and a 10 us sleep. A frame at the 6.25 MHz MDC the bootloader leaves set
takes ~10 us, so nearly every access sleeps once and pays the timer
latency on top of the transfer. That adds up on buses driven through an
indirect register window, where one logical access is dozens of frames:
the RTL8372N switch on the GL-BE9300 needs ~36 frames per PHY register
write, and its boot-time RTL8224 microcode load is thousands of those.

Spin up to 100 us first (readl_poll_timeout() with no sleep, which still
checks the clock every pass), which covers a normal frame, and only then
fall back to the existing sleeping poll with the same 10 ms timeout. A
wedged bus therefore still yields the CPU between polls, which matters
on PREEMPT_NONE kernels, and still fails with -ETIMEDOUT. This affects
every board using this driver, not just the one measured; the fast path
only ever shortens a wait.

Measured on a GL-BE9300: switch probe to DSA setup 2.89 s -> ~2.6 s.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-ipq4019.c
+++ b/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-ipq4019.c
@@ -42,6 +42,11 @@
 
 #define IPQ4019_MDIO_TIMEOUT	10000
 #define IPQ4019_MDIO_SLEEP		10
+/* A frame at the usual 6.25 MHz MDC takes ~10 us: spin that long first
+ * rather than paying timer latency on every frame, then fall back to the
+ * sleeping poll so a wedged bus still yields the CPU.
+ */
+#define IPQ4019_MDIO_SPIN_US	100
 
 /* MDIO clock source frequency is fixed to 100M */
 #define IPQ_MDIO_CLK_RATE	100000000
@@ -60,6 +65,14 @@ static int ipq4019_mdio_wait_busy(struct
 	struct ipq4019_mdio_data *priv = bus->priv;
 	unsigned int busy;
 
+	/* sleep_us = 0 tight-loops but, unlike the _atomic variant with no
+	 * delay, still bounds the spin by time.
+	 */
+	if (!readl_poll_timeout(priv->membase + MDIO_CMD_REG, busy,
+				(busy & MDIO_CMD_ACCESS_BUSY) == 0,
+				0, IPQ4019_MDIO_SPIN_US))
+		return 0;
+
 	return readl_poll_timeout(priv->membase + MDIO_CMD_REG, busy,
 				  (busy & MDIO_CMD_ACCESS_BUSY) == 0,
 				  IPQ4019_MDIO_SLEEP, IPQ4019_MDIO_TIMEOUT);
